    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d801">

                <pb facs="0893" n="798" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg798"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.6363/294: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dickover</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-11-06T15:00:00-05:00">November 6, 1936—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>140. Your despatch No. 2076, October 14, in regard to oil interests in
                        Japan.<anchor n="82" xml:id="d801-f1"/> Proposed action, as outlined in
                    concluding paragraph of your despatch under reference, has Department’s
                    approval. Department <pb facs="0894" n="799" xml:id="pg799"/> suggests that, in
                    your discretion, Embassy endeavor to obtain written rather than oral
                    confirmation of Japanese Government’s assurances.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d801-f1" n="82" xml:id="d801-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in the final paragraph the Ambassador in Japan stated that
                    confirmation of previous oral assurances by the Japanese Government had not yet
                    been given, but “there is little doubt that the confirmation will be given in
                    due course.” He suggested that the Embassy request “oral or written
                    confirmation” of a memorandum of previous assurances to be presented to the
                    Chief of the Commercial Affairs Bureau of the Japanese Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
